Working definition of the ``Opacity Crisis.''}
We use ``Opacity Crisis'' to denote the breakdown of a long-lived, hot hydrostatic atmosphere inside the soliton: 
in the same parameter space where $N_H\gtrsim 10^{24}\,{\rm cm^{-2}}$ is achievable within $r_{\rm e}\sim O(r_c)$, the effective radiative-loss or energy-redistribution time $t_{\rm rad}\equiv \max(t_{\rm cool},t_{\rm diff,es})$ is not $\gg t_{\rm dyn}$.
The system therefore cannot remain as a quasi-static hot atmosphere;
it must evolve dynamically, either via rapid cooling-driven collapse ($t_{\rm cool}\ll t_{\rm dyn}$) or into a radiation-pressure-supported optically thick inflow/envelope if IR trapping becomes important ($t_{\rm diff,IR}\gtrsim t_{\rm dyn}$).
In either case, the initial static HSE picture is invalid in the regime relevant for LRD-like obscuration.

